 2. 🏛️ Schumer&#x27;s new challenger 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 


 Sen. Chris Van Hollen speaks at Crooked Con, the "Pod Save America" convention in Washington on Saturday. Photo: Nathan Howard/AP Sen. Chris Van Hollen (D-Md.) announced last night on MS NOW's "The Briefing with Jen Psaki" that he'll challenge Chuck Schumer for majority leader.

 Van Hollen  — who, at 67, is nearly a decade younger than Schumer, who turns 76 later this month — was  immediately endorsed  by Sen.-elect Abdul El-Sayed of Michigan, who said the American people want "bold leadership." Why it matters: The attempt to oust Schumer underscores deep divisions plaguing Democrats, despite the party's midterm romp, Axios' Kathleen Hunter and Justin Green write. 

 📺 Van Hollen said his bid was " about answering the call of the American people. ... We saw a huge outburst of democracy on Election Day."

 Voters sent a message to "reject the status quo and to reject business as usual," he added. Van Hollen acknowledged he's the "underdog." But he told Psaki he has "more than 10" senators on his side.

 At least six senators-elect — El-Sayed, Adam Hamilton (Kan.), Juliana Stratton (Ill.), Peggy Flanagan (Minn.), Troy Jackson (Maine) and Annie Andrews (S.C.) — have said they won't support Schumer for leader. Share this story . 

 
 
