 5. 🥊 Unlikely MAGA allies 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Sens. Bernie Moreno (left) and Josh Hawley attend a committee meeting in August. Photo: Tom Williams/CQ-Roll Call via Getty Images Some of Senate Democrats' most surprising allies in the next Congress could hail from the right wing of the Republican Party, Axios' Stef Kight reports. 

 Why it matters: Democrats are on track to need only four GOP votes to break filibusters next Congress. They could find those votes from a bloc of GOP senators with a shared appetite for taking on corporate power. 👀 Enter Sens. Josh Hawley (R-Mo.), Jim Banks (R-Ind.), Bernie Moreno (R-Ohio) and Eric Schmitt (R-Mo.).

 Each has already worked with Democrats, and their votes could put legislation over the top. Zoom in: Hawley has co-led multiple pieces of legislation with progressive star Sen. Elizabeth Warren (D-Mass.) this Congress.

 He's been scathing in his post-election critiques, telling reporters this week: "This Congress did not come and work for the people. This Congress came and worked for AI companies, and it worked for data centers, and it worked for big tech companies." Keep reading . 

 
 
